{#- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Shared markup fragments.

   pageHeader() replaces a 200-character <section> line that was copy-pasted
   into eight page templates verbatim. That duplication was not merely untidy:
   every one of the eight carried the same unsanitised `photoPosition` straight
   into a style attribute, so a single fix had to be applied eight times and
   would silently miss any page added later. Now the banner exists once.

   `header` is the matching entry from src/_data/pageHeaders.json (the CMS's
   "Page Header Photos"). A page with no entry there renders the plain navy
   gradient header, which is the correct fallback rather than an error.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------- #}
{% macro pageHeader(header, title, subtitle) %}
{#- cssUrl, not the raw value: the path goes inside url('...') in a style
    attribute, and HTML-escaping does not protect a CSS context. Filtering here
    means `photo` below is either a real upload path or empty, so the same
    variable can gate both the class and the attribute. #}
{%- set photo = (header.photo if header else null) | cssUrl %}
<section class="page-header{% if photo %} page-header--photo{% endif %}"{% if photo %} style="background-image:url('{{ photo }}'); background-position:{{ header.photoPosition | cssPosition }};"{% endif %}>
  <div class="container">
    <h1>{{ title }}</h1>
    {%- if subtitle %}
    <p>{{ subtitle }}</p>
    {%- endif %}
  </div>
</section>
{% endmacro %}
